So, if you're looking for someone to do a code review or help you&nbsp;hunt down a bug, please consider using me.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.203
Okay, let's get back to multiple assignments.

00:00:54.203 --> 00:00:56.498
If I&nbsp;go ahead and run this code,

00:00:56.498 --> 00:01:00.895
of course, we see that four copies of the empty list are printed out.

00:01:00.895 --> 00:01:05.909
But&nbsp;are these the same copy of the empty list or are they different copies of the empty list?

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:12.146
So, if I&nbsp;print out a is b, do you think this is going to be true or false?

00:01:12.146 --> 00:01:15.727
Let's run it again and we see that&nbsp;the answer is True.

00:01:15.727 --> 00:01:21.340
So, these are in fact all just different names for the same copy of the empty&nbsp;list.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.169
They're not all different empty lists.

00:01:23.169 --> 00:01:27.304
This is slightly different than what you would have if you&nbsp;did a tuple unpacking.

00:01:27.304 --> 00:01:30.749
If you said, like, a, b = [ ], [ ],

00:01:30.749 --> 00:01:34.429
then these two&nbsp;empty lists on the right hand side are different.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:37.931
So, a and b will be pointing to different things.

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:43.638
So, when I run it this time, I see False at the end because these two empty lists are different.

00:01:43.638 --> 00:01:46.302
What's&nbsp;happening here is analogous to the following.

00:01:46.302 --> 00:01:50.751
First, we evaluate the right hand side and store&nbsp;it in a temporary variable.

00:01:50.751 --> 00:01:52.627
Then we go from left to right

00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:57.000
and assign each of the things equal&nbsp;to the temporary variable one after the other.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.940
When we write it this way, it's clear that they're&nbsp;all going to be both equal

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:08.617
but also identical in the sense that a is b will be True.

00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:12.982
Thinking about&nbsp;it this way is really going to help us understand this trickier example.

00:02:12.982 --> 00:02:17.280
For starters, let's go&nbsp;ahead and print out what we get in this example.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.374
If you'd like a moment to think about it for&nbsp;yourself before I explain, pause the video now.

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:24.857
And make sure that you're able to&nbsp;explain why this [...] appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.520
Okay, here's how you're supposed to reason about&nbsp;this code.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.644
First off, we make a temporary variable and set it equal to the right hand side.

00:02:34.644 --> 00:02:36.649
Then&nbsp;we go from left to right.

00:02:36.649 --> 00:02:39.128
And we do each of the assignments one after another.

00:02:39.128 --> 00:02:45.142
So, I'll say a comma&nbsp;b equals tmp and then a slice equals tmp.

00:02:45.142 --> 00:02:49.508
After this assignment, we have that a is equal to this&nbsp;list containing the empty list

00:02:49.508 --> 00:02:51.819
and b is equal to this empty list.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:55.753
So, in particular, after this line,&nbsp;a is a list.

00:02:55.753 --> 00:03:00.817
Since a is a list after the previous line, it makes sense to use this expression.

00:03:00.817 --> 00:03:06.000
So, what&nbsp;this does is, it assigns the contents of a equal to the right hand side.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.480
So, the contents of a will&nbsp;become equal to the contents of this tuple.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.159
Meaning that, a will now contain these two elements.

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:19.440
But remember, before this line, a was equal to this element.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.236
So, then when we're setting its contents&nbsp;to be these two elements,

00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:26.738
we're setting a to be one of the members of itself.

00:03:26.738 --> 00:03:28.726
that's where the [...] come in.

00:03:28.726 --> 00:03:32.539
When you print out a recursive cyclic data structure like that,

00:03:32.539 --> 00:03:34.789
Python will notice that&nbsp;there's a cycle.

00:03:34.789 --> 00:03:36.386
And then just print [...]

00:03:36.386 --> 00:03:39.620
And that's your cue to think: Oh, I have a&nbsp;cyclic reference in there.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.693
So, let's go ahead and print it out again and see what we have.

00:03:43.693 --> 00:03:47.359
So, we&nbsp;started with a equal to this and b equal to this.

00:03:47.359 --> 00:03:52.150
And then we replace the contents of a with this&nbsp;and this, which are a and b.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:56.166
So, you can see here, the highlighted blue portion. That's the contents of a.

00:03:56.166 --> 00:04:00.880
So, a contains a and b. That's why we have these [...] here.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.040
It can't just print out all of a. Because&nbsp;of the cyclic reference, it would go on forever.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:10.529
We can confirm our intuition by printing out&nbsp;a is a[0].

00:04:10.529 --> 00:04:12.477
Since this is going to be true

00:04:12.477 --> 00:04:15.625
that shows us that the first element of a is a,

00:04:15.625 --> 00:04:17.911
confirming that we have this cyclic reference.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.365
Okay, I admit that example was kind of silly.

00:04:21.365 --> 00:04:23.826
You&nbsp;really shouldn't ever be writing code like this.

00:04:23.826 --> 00:04:26.056
I think a good rule of thumb is that

00:04:26.056 --> 00:04:31.269
for any&nbsp;variable like a, you shouldn't use it in more than one part of a multiple assignment.

00:04:31.269 --> 00:04:33.896
Otherwise, you're&nbsp;just asking to confuse people.

00:04:33.896 --> 00:04:37.706
So, yeah. You might be able to use this to trick your friends or whatever.

00:04:37.706 --> 00:04:41.034
But in real code, please don't ever do this.

00:04:41.034 --> 00:04:45.840
But I think it is important to understand&nbsp;the way that multiple assignments work.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.120
Namely that, you first evaluate the right hand side&nbsp;and then go left to right assigning things in turn.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.664
So, this is really the example that you should keep&nbsp;in mind.

00:04:54.664 --> 00:04:58.893
If the only language you're familiar with is Python, this might not be confusing at all.

00:04:58.893 --> 00:05:02.146
But&nbsp;a lot of you I know do know more than one language.

00:05:02.146 --> 00:05:07.360
And you might actually be confused by this because&nbsp;a lot of languages do it a slightly different way.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.774
One big difference about Python is that:

00:05:09.774 --> 00:05:14.744
In&nbsp;Python, an assignment is not an expression, it's a statement.

00:05:14.744 --> 00:05:20.538
The difference here being that&nbsp;something like d equals the empty string doesn't return anything.

00:05:20.538 --> 00:05:21.815
It doesn't have a value.

00:05:21.815 --> 00:05:25.336
It just&nbsp;assigns the right hand side to the left hand side.

00:05:25.336 --> 00:05:29.347
But in a lot of other languages, assignments are&nbsp;also expressions.

00:05:29.347 --> 00:05:36.043
So, the assignment d equals empty list would return d back to you.

00:05:36.043 --> 00:05:41.244
But Python does&nbsp;have assignment expressions. They were added in 3.8.

00:05:41.244 --> 00:05:43.389
They use this colon equal syntax.

00:05:43.389 --> 00:05:49.274
And it does&nbsp;essentially the same thing that the equals does with some restrictions about where you can use it.

00:05:49.274 --> 00:05:52.154
But it also returns the value back to you.

00:05:52.154 --> 00:05:56.573
So, this d := 0 is going to return that zero&nbsp;back to me

00:05:56.573 --> 00:06:01.118
which I then assign to c and then to b and then to a.

00:06:01.118 --> 00:06:02.917
And when you write it this way,

00:06:02.917 --> 00:06:09.763
it&nbsp;becomes clear that the only way that this could make sense is if you do things right to left, not&nbsp;left to right.

00:06:09.763 --> 00:06:12.624
I have to start on the right hand side here.

00:06:12.624 --> 00:06:15.485
Otherwise, the parentheses can't make&nbsp;sense.

00:06:15.485 --> 00:06:20.359
Because I couldn't start on the left with a equals b, because b isn't defined yet.

00:06:20.359 --> 00:06:22.058
I have&nbsp;to start right to left.

00:06:23.047 --> 00:06:26.557
So, that's going to be the big difference between Python and other languages.

00:06:26.557 --> 00:06:30.436
In Python, the normal equals operator is going to go left to right.

00:06:30.436 --> 00:06:35.452
But in many other languages and&nbsp;in Python if you're using assignment expressions,

00:06:35.452 --> 00:06:37.458
then it actually goes right to left.

00:06:37.458 --> 00:06:42.201
And if you're&nbsp;wondering about all the redundant parentheses here, they're there because they have to be.

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:47.712
You're not&nbsp;allowed to chain multiple assignment expressions together unless you put them in parentheses.

00:06:47.712 --> 00:06:51.579
Python does this to discourage people from using assignment expressions

00:06:51.579 --> 00:06:55.780
where they really&nbsp;should be using just regular old equal sign.

